<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Midas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. late 8th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> · Also known as Mita of Mushki, King Midas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Midas was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> in the late 8th century BCE, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordion</a>. Assyrian records call him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a> of Mushki and show him as a rival and later a correspondent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a>. He married a princess of Cyme and was said to be the first foreigner to send an offering to Delphi. Greek tradition says he killed himself when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> overran Phrygia, and made him the king with the golden touch.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/midas.jpg" alt="Midas" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Midas Painter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red-figure_stamnos_Silenos_Mida_440BC_Cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Several kings named Midas ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>, and later tradition merged them. The oldest, who belongs to myth, was said to be the son of Gordias and the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cybele">Cybele</a>, or in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>'s account the son of Gordios, a poor peasant. Arrian tells that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygians">Phrygians</a>, torn by civil discord, were told by an oracle that a wagon would bring them a king.</p><p>Midas arrived in a wagon with his parents, was made king and dedicated the wagon in the citadel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, giving rise to the saying that whoever loosened its knot would rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-roman-province">Asia</a>. Legends place this Midas before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> does not mention him. A second Midas of the late 8th century BCE is the historical figure, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> names a third, of the 6th century BCE, grandfather of Adrastus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Midas of the late 8th century BCE ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a> until the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>. Most historians identify him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mita">Mita</a>, king of the Mushki, who according to Assyrian tablets of the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-ii">Sargon II</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>'s eastern Anatolian provinces. Some historians think he was called king of the Mushki because he had subjected that people and taken them into his army.</p><p>Greek sources say that he married Damodice, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> of Cyme, and traded extensively with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. Herodotus records that a Midas son of Gordias gave a royal throne to the oracle at Delphi and was the only foreigner to give an offering there before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyges-of-lydia">Gyges of Lydia</a>. Some historians believe that this was the 8th-century king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Greek writers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, say that Midas killed himself by drinking bull's blood during the Cimmerian attack, which Eusebius dated to about 695 BCE and Julius Africanus to about 676 BCE. Excavation has confirmed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gordion">Gordium</a> was destroyed and burned at about that time. In 1957 Rodney Young opened a royal burial in the Great Tumulus at Gordion, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> cut around 740 BCE, a collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> drinking vessels and inlaid tables. It was named Tumulus MM, for Midas Mound, and is now generally thought to cover his father.</p><p>Myth gave Midas the golden touch, granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> in thanks for his kindness to Silenus and lifted when he washed in the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pactolus">Pactolus</a>, whose sands turned to gold. In another story <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> gave him donkey's ears when he preferred Pan's music, and his barber's whispered secret grew into reeds that repeated it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Midas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to Strabo and others, <b>Midas</b> killed himself by drinking bulls' blood when the Cimmerians overran his kingdom and sacked Gordium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In the west, Dugdammî destroyed Phrygia around c. 675 BCE, after which its king <b>Midas</b> killed himself, and by the early 660s BC the Cimmerians were masters of Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>They overran Phrygia around 696 BCE, when <b>King Midas</b> is said to have killed himself, and later sacked Sardis, killing Gyges of Lydia around 644 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a></li><li><blockquote>A Cymaean princess was said to have married <b>King Midas</b> of Phrygia.</blockquote><a href="/state/cyme-aeolis">Cyme (Aeolis)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in crouton. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
